1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:10,300
This is how you work with Murmurent: you type to Claude Code in plain English. Every other movie assumes these two minutes.

2
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:22,399
You open your project folder, which is already set up for Murmurent. The agents and the shared rules arrive once, with the install, so every session has them.

3
00:00:22,399 --> 00:00:29,699
Then you start Claude Code in that folder. From then on, you type English.

4
00:00:30,099 --> 00:00:49,799
Ask for what you want in plain English, and Claude Code does the work. When you want a specialist, name it: ask the bookworm for papers, ask the adversary to check an analysis, ask the oracle to remember a finding. Each agent has one job.

5
00:00:50,199 --> 00:00:57,099
Here is a real exchange. The member names the security guard and says what to check.

6
00:00:57,099 --> 00:01:08,399
The first line of every reply is the answer. Here it is Blocked. Read that line first; the detail below says where and why.

7
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:23,700
Each agent's first line also appears in a small window as it finishes. Keep that window in a corner of the screen, and you can follow the agents without reading their full replies.

8
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:41,799
Your work is kept. Every request you type is kept in your notebook's record of sessions, except one that looks like patient details or a password. Ask the oracle to remember a finding, and it keeps it for the next time you ask.

9
00:01:41,799 --> 00:01:48,700
And inside Claude Code, agents are stopped from changing your lab's original data.

10
00:01:49,100 --> 00:02:00,799
Three habits matter. Name the agent and give it the files themselves. Read the first line of the reply. And ask the oracle to remember what you find.

11
00:02:00,799 --> 00:02:08,599
Each agent has its own short movie that shows it this way.
